Task 2: Emotion Recognition from Speech

CodeAlpha Machine Learning Internship

Objective

Recognize emotional categories in speech audio using audio signal processing and machine learning.

Method

1. Read speech WAV files.
2. Extract 40 MFCC coefficients using Librosa.
3. Calculate the mean and standard deviation of each coefficient (80 features per audio clip).
4. Train a Random Forest or Support Vector Machine (SVM).
5. Evaluate using accuracy, precision, recall, F1-score and a confusion matrix.

Dataset: RAVDESS

Download the RAVDESS Audio-only dataset from the official Ryerson Audio-Visual Database of Emotional Speech and Song page:
https://zenodo.org/records/1188976

Extract the audio files under data/ravdess/. Keep the original RAVDESS filenames and folder structure. The script recursively finds .wav files and reads emotion labels from the third filename field.

Supported RAVDESS labels:

• 01 neutral
• 02 calm
• 03 happy
• 04 sad
• 05 angry
• 06 fearful
• 07 disgust
• 08 surprised

Install and train

python -m venv .venv
# Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
# macOS/Linux: source .venv/bin/activate
pip install -r requirements.txt

python main.py --data data/ravdess --classifier random_forest
# Alternative:
python main.py --data data/ravdess --classifier svm

Predict one audio file

After training:

python main.py --predict path/to/audio.wav

Important

• Training requires the RAVDESS WAV dataset; it is not bundled because it is a large third-party dataset.
• Results depend on the dataset, speaker split and recording conditions. A random file-level split can place clips from the same actor in both train and test sets; for a stronger experiment, evaluate with actor-independent splitting.
• This is an educational demo. Speech emotion labels are subjective and model predictions should not be treated as reliable assessments of a person’s actual emotional state.

